import json

with open('complete-qa.json') as f:
    data = json.load(f)

def write_q(idx, z3_secs, sp, kp):
    q = data['questions'][idx]
    q['answer']['sections'] = z3_secs + [
        {"type":"speakable_answer","title":"How to Answer This Verbally","content":sp},
        {"type":"key_points","title":"Key Points","content":kp}
    ]

# Q6: OneToMany Fetch Strategies — comparison, complexity 3
write_q(6, [
    {"type":"overview","title":"EAGER @OneToMany Doesn't Fix N+1 \u2014 It Creates a Cartesian Product Instead",
     "content":"Developers see N+1 queries on a `@OneToMany` collection and reach for `FetchType.EAGER`, expecting Hibernate to load everything in one query. It does \u2014 with a JOIN that **multiplies result rows.** If an order has 10 items, the JOIN returns 10 rows per order. Load 100 orders and you get 1,000 rows \u2014 100 order copies with their items inlined. With two EAGER collections, the Cartesian product explodes further.\n\nHibernate offers three fetch strategies for `@OneToMany` that control how the collection is loaded: **SELECT** (the default \u2014 one query per collection, causing N+1), **JOIN** (one query with a JOIN \u2014 risks Cartesian products and breaks pagination), and **SUBSELECT** (one additional query with a subselect \u2014 no Cartesian, no N+1, but loads all matching collections at once).\n\nThe right strategy depends on whether you need pagination, how many collections you're loading, and whether loading all collections at once is acceptable. There's no universally correct choice."},
    {"type":"comparison_table","title":"SELECT vs JOIN vs SUBSELECT \u2014 When Each Strategy Wins",
     "content":"Each strategy trades off between query count, result size, and pagination compatibility.\n\n| Strategy | Queries | Pagination | Cartesian Risk | Best For |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **SELECT** (default) | 1 + N | Works | None | Small N, simple use cases |\n| **JOIN** (`@Fetch(JOIN)`) | 1 | Breaks \u2014 in-memory pagination | Yes \u2014 rows multiply | Single collection, no pagination |\n| **SUBSELECT** (`@Fetch(SUBSELECT)`) | 2 | Works | None | Large N, single collection |\n| **@BatchSize(size=25)** | 1 + N/25 | Works | None | Pagination + multiple collections |\n\n**JOIN fetch breaks pagination** because Hibernate must load all joined rows into memory to deduplicate and then apply `firstResult/maxResults` in Java. The HHH90003004 warning signals this. SUBSELECT avoids this by issuing a separate query with the parent IDs as a subselect, loading all matching child collections in one shot.\n\n**@BatchSize is the pragmatic choice** for most production scenarios \u2014 it reduces query count without Cartesian products and works with pagination. A batch size of 25-50 balances between query count and IN clause size."},
],
    "The three fetch strategies for `@OneToMany` \u2014 SELECT, JOIN, and SUBSELECT \u2014 each solve N+1 differently with different trade-offs.\n\nSELECT is the default: one query per collection, classic N+1. JOIN loads everything in one query but **creates a Cartesian product** \u2014 10 items per order means 10 rows per order. With two EAGER collections, the product explodes. JOIN also breaks pagination \u2014 Hibernate loads all rows into memory and paginates in Java.\n\nSUBSELECT issues exactly two queries: one for parents, one for all children using the parent query as a subselect. No Cartesian, works with pagination, but loads all matching collections at once.\n\n`@BatchSize(size = 25)` is the pragmatic choice for most cases \u2014 it reduces N+1 to N/25+1, works with pagination, and handles multiple collections without Cartesian products.\n\nIn practice, I use `@BatchSize` as the default and only switch to SUBSELECT when I know the full collection set is needed.",
    "- **EAGER @OneToMany creates Cartesian products, not clean joins** \u2014 10 items per order means 10 rows per order in the result set; with two EAGER collections the product multiplies further.\n- **JOIN fetch breaks pagination with in-memory processing** \u2014 Hibernate loads all joined rows, deduplicates in Java, then applies firstResult/maxResults; warning HHH90003004 signals this.\n- **SUBSELECT loads all matching collections in exactly two queries** \u2014 uses the parent query as a subselect for child loading; no Cartesian product and works with pagination.\n- **@BatchSize(size=25) reduces N+1 to N/25+1 and works everywhere** \u2014 batches child IDs into `WHERE id IN (...)` queries; no Cartesian, no pagination issues, handles multiple collections.\n- **Multiple EAGER collections cause a Cartesian product explosion** \u2014 two collections with 10 items each produce 100 rows per parent; Hibernate warns but doesn't prevent it."
)

# Q7: EntityGraph — how-to-recipe, complexity 3
write_q(7, [
    {"type":"overview","title":"@EntityGraph Is Spring Data's Declarative Alternative to JOIN FETCH \u2014 With the Same Pagination Trap",
     "content":"Writing `JOIN FETCH` JPQL queries for every repository method gets repetitive. **`@EntityGraph` lets you declare which associations to fetch eagerly** on a per-query basis, directly on Spring Data repository method signatures, without writing JPQL.\n\nUnder the hood, `@EntityGraph` generates a LEFT JOIN \u2014 the same SQL as `JOIN FETCH`. This means it carries the **same pagination caveat**: combining `@EntityGraph` with `Pageable` on a collection association causes Hibernate to load all rows into memory and paginate in Java, logging the HHH90003004 warning.\n\nThere are two graph types: **FETCH** (attributes in the graph are loaded eagerly, all others follow their default fetch type) and **LOAD** (attributes in the graph are loaded eagerly, all others follow their **entity-level** fetch type \u2014 if an association is marked EAGER on the entity, it stays EAGER even if not in the graph). The distinction matters when you want `@EntityGraph` to override EAGER associations to LAZY \u2014 only FETCH type does that."},
    {"type":"step","title":"Ad-hoc vs Named EntityGraphs \u2014 When Reuse Justifies the Extra Annotation",
     "content":"For simple cases, **ad-hoc `attributePaths`** directly on the repository method is the cleanest approach. No entity annotation needed.\n\n```java\npublic interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {\n    @EntityGraph(attributePaths = {\"customer\", \"items\"})\n    List<Order> findByStatus(String status);\n}\n```\n\nThis tells Hibernate to LEFT JOIN `customer` and `items` when loading orders by status. The same repository can have another method without `@EntityGraph` that loads orders lazily \u2014 **each method controls its own fetch strategy.**\n\nFor graphs reused across multiple repositories or services, **`@NamedEntityGraph`** on the entity class is cleaner. Define the graph once on the entity, reference it by name in repository methods. This avoids duplicating `attributePaths` strings across multiple methods.\n\n**For paginated queries with collections**, avoid `@EntityGraph` entirely. Use `@BatchSize` on the association instead, or split the query into two: first load parent IDs with pagination, then load the full entities with `@EntityGraph` using the ID list."},
],
    "`@EntityGraph` is Spring Data's way to control fetch strategy per query without writing JPQL. Put `@EntityGraph(attributePaths = {\"customer\", \"items\"})` on a repository method and Hibernate generates a LEFT JOIN for those associations.\n\nThe critical gotcha is that it has the **same pagination problem as JOIN FETCH.** Combining `@EntityGraph` with `Pageable` on a collection causes Hibernate to load everything into memory and paginate in Java. Use `@BatchSize` for paginated queries instead.\n\nThere are two graph types: FETCH and LOAD. **FETCH** loads graph attributes eagerly and everything else lazily. **LOAD** loads graph attributes eagerly but keeps entity-level EAGER annotations active. Use FETCH when you want full control.\n\nFor simple cases, ad-hoc `attributePaths` on the repository method is enough. For reuse across repositories, use `@NamedEntityGraph` on the entity.\n\nIn practice, I use `@EntityGraph` for non-paginated queries and `@BatchSize` for paginated ones. Mixing them avoids both N+1 and the in-memory pagination trap.",
    "- **@EntityGraph generates LEFT JOIN \u2014 same SQL as JOIN FETCH** \u2014 it's a declarative alternative for Spring Data repositories; no JPQL needed but same behavior under the hood.\n- **Pagination + @EntityGraph on collections loads everything into memory** \u2014 Hibernate applies firstResult/maxResults in Java, not SQL; use @BatchSize for paginated collection fetching.\n- **FETCH graph type overrides entity-level EAGER to LAZY** \u2014 only attributes in the graph are fetched eagerly; LOAD type preserves entity-level EAGER annotations for attributes not in the graph.\n- **Ad-hoc attributePaths avoids entity annotation clutter** \u2014 `@EntityGraph(attributePaths = \"customer\")` on the repository method is sufficient for one-off fetch overrides; use @NamedEntityGraph only for cross-repository reuse.\n- **Split paginated queries to avoid the JOIN trap** \u2014 first query loads parent IDs with Pageable, second query loads full entities with @EntityGraph using the ID list."
)

# Q8: LazyInitializationException — debugging-pattern, complexity 3
write_q(8, [
    {"type":"overview","title":"LazyInitializationException Is a Symptom \u2014 The Real Problem Is Where Your Transaction Boundary Ends",
     "content":"`LazyInitializationException` happens when code accesses a lazy-loaded association after the persistence context has closed. **The entity is detached, the Hibernate session is gone, and the proxy can't load the data.** Most developers encounter this when a controller or view template accesses a lazy collection that was loaded in a service method's `@Transactional` scope.\n\nThe common \"fix\" is `FetchType.EAGER` \u2014 which silences the exception but causes over-fetching in every query that touches the entity. The other common fix is OSIV (Open Session in View) \u2014 Spring Boot enables it by default \u2014 which keeps the session open during response rendering, allowing lazy loads during JSON serialization. **OSIV masks the problem while silently firing N+1 queries in the view layer.**\n\nThe actual fix is ensuring all required data is loaded within the transaction boundary \u2014 using `JOIN FETCH`, `@EntityGraph`, `Hibernate.initialize()`, or DTO projections that select only the columns needed."},
    {"type":"step","title":"Three Proper Fixes \u2014 And Why OSIV Is an Anti-Pattern in REST APIs",
     "content":"**Fix 1: JOIN FETCH or @EntityGraph** loads the association in the original query. This is the best approach when you know which associations the caller needs. Each service method or repository query fetches exactly what its use case requires.\n\n**Fix 2: `Hibernate.initialize(entity.getCollection())`** forces loading within the transaction. Use this as a targeted workaround when you can't modify the query \u2014 for example, when a shared repository method is used by multiple callers with different fetch needs. It fires an additional SELECT but keeps the lazy association otherwise untouched.\n\n**Fix 3: DTO projections** bypass the problem entirely. `SELECT new OrderSummary(o.id, o.status, c.name) FROM Order o JOIN o.customer c` returns a plain object with exactly the columns needed. No entity management, no lazy proxies, no persistence context overhead.\n\n**Disable OSIV** with `spring.jpa.open-in-view=false` for REST APIs. OSIV was designed for server-rendered views where templates access entity properties. In a REST API, Jackson serializing entities fires lazy loads during response writing \u2014 outside your control, outside your monitoring, and with no way to optimize the queries."},
],
    "LazyInitializationException happens when you access a lazy collection after the persistence context closes \u2014 typically outside the `@Transactional` method that loaded the entity.\n\nThe wrong fixes are common. **EAGER loading** silences the exception but over-fetches in every query. **OSIV** (enabled by default in Spring Boot) keeps the session open during response rendering, masking the problem while firing N+1 queries during JSON serialization.\n\nThe right fixes depend on the situation. **JOIN FETCH or @EntityGraph** loads what you need in the original query. **`Hibernate.initialize()`** forces loading within the transaction when you can't modify the query. **DTO projections** bypass the problem entirely \u2014 no entities, no lazy proxies.\n\nDisable OSIV with `spring.jpa.open-in-view=false` for REST APIs. It was designed for server-rendered views, not for Jackson serialization where lazy loads fire outside your control.\n\nIn practice, DTO projections are the cleanest solution \u2014 they eliminate both the exception and the performance overhead of entity management.",
    "- **LazyInitializationException means the persistence context is already closed** \u2014 the entity is detached and the proxy can't execute the lazy-load SQL; the fix is loading data within the transaction, not extending the session.\n- **OSIV masks the problem by keeping the session open during response rendering** \u2014 `spring.jpa.open-in-view=true` (default) allows lazy loads during JSON serialization; fires uncontrolled N+1 queries in the view layer.\n- **EAGER loading silences the exception but over-fetches everywhere** \u2014 every query touching the entity loads the collection; Cartesian products with multiple EAGER collections.\n- **Hibernate.initialize() forces loading within the transaction** \u2014 fires an additional SELECT for the lazy collection; use when you can't modify the query but need the data before the transaction closes.\n- **DTO projections bypass the problem entirely** \u2014 `SELECT new Dto(...)` returns plain objects with no persistence context, no lazy proxies, and no risk of LazyInitializationException."
)

# Q9: @Transactional Internals — internals, complexity 4
write_q(9, [
    {"type":"overview","title":"@Transactional Works Through Proxies \u2014 Which Is Why Calling It From the Same Class Does Nothing",
     "content":"The most common `@Transactional` bug is self-invocation. A method in a service class calls another `@Transactional` method in the same class, expecting a transaction to start. **Nothing happens.** The transaction annotation is silently ignored because `@Transactional` works through **CGLIB proxies** \u2014 Spring creates a subclass that wraps the transactional method with `begin`, `commit`, and `rollback` logic. When you call a method on `this`, you bypass the proxy entirely.\n\nUnderstanding the proxy mechanism explains every `@Transactional` gotcha: why it only works on public methods (the proxy can't override private/final methods), why self-invocation skips the transaction, and why the annotation on an interface method may not be inherited.\n\nBeyond the proxy, the **propagation level** determines what happens when one transactional method calls another. `REQUIRED` (the default) joins the existing transaction. `REQUIRES_NEW` suspends it and creates a new one. Getting propagation wrong either silently shares transactions that should be independent or creates unnecessary transaction overhead."},
    {"type":"phase","title":"Proxy Interception \u2014 What Spring Actually Does When You Call a @Transactional Method",
     "content":"When Spring encounters a `@Transactional` bean, it creates a CGLIB proxy subclass at startup. The proxy overrides each transactional method with logic that starts a transaction before the method body and commits or rolls back after.\n\nThe call chain is: **caller \u2192 proxy \u2192 actual method \u2192 proxy (commit/rollback).** If the caller is a different bean, the call goes through the proxy. If the caller is the same class (`this.method()`), the proxy is bypassed \u2014 the call goes directly to the actual method, and Spring never sees the `@Transactional` annotation.\n\nThe fix for self-invocation is either extracting the method to a separate bean (cleanest), injecting the proxy into itself (`@Autowired private MyService self;`), or using `AopContext.currentProxy()` (least clean). In practice, extracting to a separate service is always the right answer \u2014 self-invocation usually signals that the class has too many responsibilities."},
    {"type":"phase","title":"Propagation Levels \u2014 REQUIRED, REQUIRES_NEW, and the Ones That Actually Matter",
     "content":"`REQUIRED` (the default) joins the calling transaction if one exists, or creates a new one if not. This is correct for 95% of use cases. **The subtlety: if the inner method throws a runtime exception, the outer transaction is marked for rollback** even if the outer method catches the exception. The transaction is poisoned.\n\n`REQUIRES_NEW` suspends the current transaction and creates a completely independent one. The inner transaction can commit even if the outer rolls back. Use this for **audit logging** \u2014 you want the audit entry to persist even if the business operation fails.\n\n`MANDATORY` requires an existing transaction and throws if none exists. Use this on methods that should never be called without a transaction \u2014 it catches bugs at development time.\n\n**`readOnly = true`** is not a propagation level but a critical hint. It tells the JDBC driver and Hibernate to optimize for read-only access. Hibernate skips dirty checking for entities loaded in a read-only transaction, which matters when loading thousands of entities for reporting."},
],
    "The critical thing about `@Transactional` is that it works through **CGLIB proxies.** Spring creates a subclass that wraps your method with begin/commit/rollback. When you call a `@Transactional` method from within the same class, you bypass the proxy \u2014 no transaction starts.\n\nThis explains every gotcha: it only works on public methods, self-invocation skips it, and the fix is always extracting the method to a separate bean.\n\nFor propagation, `REQUIRED` (the default) joins the existing transaction. The subtlety is that if the inner method throws, the outer transaction is **marked for rollback even if you catch the exception.** `REQUIRES_NEW` creates an independent transaction \u2014 use it for audit logs that must persist regardless.\n\n`readOnly = true` tells Hibernate to skip dirty checking for loaded entities \u2014 real performance benefit when loading thousands of entities for reports.\n\nIn practice, I always put `@Transactional(readOnly = true)` on read-heavy service methods and use `REQUIRES_NEW` only for operations that must survive parent rollback.",
    "- **Self-invocation bypasses the proxy \u2014 @Transactional is silently ignored** \u2014 calling `this.method()` skips the CGLIB proxy; extract the method to a separate bean or inject the proxy into itself.\n- **Inner exception poisons the outer transaction even if caught** \u2014 a runtime exception in a REQUIRED inner method marks the transaction for rollback; catching it in the outer method doesn't save it.\n- **REQUIRES_NEW creates an independent transaction** \u2014 the inner transaction can commit even if the outer rolls back; use for audit logging or operations that must persist regardless of parent outcome.\n- **readOnly=true skips dirty checking for all loaded entities** \u2014 Hibernate doesn't take snapshots or compare fields at flush; real performance benefit when loading thousands of entities for reports.\n- **@Transactional only works on public methods** \u2014 CGLIB proxies can't override private or final methods; annotating them compiles but the transaction is silently ignored at runtime."
)

# Q10: Optimistic vs Pessimistic Locking — comparison, complexity 3
write_q(10, [
    {"type":"overview","title":"Optimistic Locking Doesn't Lock Anything \u2014 It Detects Conflicts After the Fact",
     "content":"The names are misleading. **Optimistic locking never acquires a database lock.** It adds a `@Version` column to the entity and checks it at UPDATE time: `UPDATE order SET status = 'SHIPPED', version = 3 WHERE id = 1 AND version = 2`. If another transaction already changed the row (version is now 3), the WHERE clause matches zero rows, and Hibernate throws `OptimisticLockException`. **Your application must catch this and retry** \u2014 JPA doesn't retry for you.\n\n**Pessimistic locking acquires a real database lock** with `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE`. No other transaction can modify the row until the lock is released at commit. This guarantees no conflicts but **holds the lock for the entire transaction duration**, blocking other transactions and potentially causing deadlocks.\n\nThe choice depends on conflict frequency. Optimistic locking works when conflicts are rare \u2014 most updates succeed, and the occasional retry is acceptable. Pessimistic locking works when conflicts are frequent and retries would cascade into more conflicts."},
    {"type":"comparison_table","title":"Optimistic vs Pessimistic \u2014 Conflict Handling, Performance, and Connection Impact",
     "content":"The trade-off is between conflict prevention (pessimistic) and conflict detection (optimistic).\n\n| Aspect | Optimistic | Pessimistic |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Mechanism** | `@Version` column, check at UPDATE time | `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` database lock |\n| **Lock held** | None | For entire transaction duration |\n| **On conflict** | `OptimisticLockException` \u2014 app must retry | Blocks until lock released |\n| **Deadlock risk** | None | Yes \u2014 two transactions locking rows in different order |\n| **Throughput** | High \u2014 no blocking | Lower \u2014 serializes access |\n| **Connection pool impact** | Minimal | Significant \u2014 connections held longer |\n| **Best for** | Low contention, read-heavy | High contention, short critical sections |\n\n**Connection pool impact is the hidden cost of pessimistic locking.** A locked row holds a database connection for the entire transaction. If many transactions lock rows simultaneously, the connection pool (default HikariCP `maximumPoolSize = 10`) exhausts quickly, and new requests queue or timeout. Size your connection pool based on expected concurrent locked transactions."},
],
    "The names are confusing. **Optimistic locking doesn't lock anything** \u2014 it uses a `@Version` column and checks it at UPDATE time. If another transaction changed the row, Hibernate throws `OptimisticLockException` and your application must catch it and retry.\n\nPessimistic locking acquires a real `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` database lock. No conflicts possible, but connections are held for the entire transaction, which hits your connection pool hard.\n\nOptimistic works when conflicts are rare \u2014 most updates succeed, and retries are occasional. Pessimistic works when conflicts are frequent and retries would cascade.\n\nFor the `@Version` field, use `Integer` or `Long`, not `int` \u2014 null initial value signals a new entity. Spring Retry's `@Retryable` automates the retry loop for `OptimisticLockException`.\n\nIn practice, optimistic locking with retry is correct for 90% of cases. Pessimistic locking should be limited to short critical sections where conflict is near-certain.",
    "- **Optimistic locking doesn't acquire database locks** \u2014 the `@Version` column is checked at UPDATE time; `WHERE version = :expected` matches zero rows if another transaction already committed.\n- **OptimisticLockException requires application-level retry** \u2014 JPA doesn't retry automatically; use Spring Retry's `@Retryable(OptimisticLockException.class)` with backoff for automatic retry.\n- **Pessimistic locking holds connections for the entire transaction** \u2014 `SELECT FOR UPDATE` keeps the database connection locked; HikariCP's default `maximumPoolSize=10` exhausts quickly under high concurrency.\n- **Use Integer/Long for @Version, not int** \u2014 null initial value on wrapper types signals a new entity to Hibernate; primitive `int` defaults to 0 which can cause incorrect version checks.\n- **Pessimistic locking risks deadlocks** \u2014 two transactions locking rows in different order block each other; set `javax.persistence.lock.timeout` to fail fast instead of waiting indefinitely."
)

# Q11: Inheritance Mapping — comparison, complexity 3
write_q(11, [
    {"type":"overview","title":"SINGLE_TABLE Is Fast but Makes Every Subclass Column Nullable \u2014 That's the Fundamental Trade-off",
     "content":"JPA supports three inheritance mapping strategies, each with a different SQL representation. **SINGLE_TABLE** puts all subclasses in one table with a discriminator column \u2014 fast polymorphic queries (no JOINs) but every subclass-specific column must be nullable. **JOINED** puts shared fields in a parent table and subclass fields in separate tables \u2014 normalized, allows NOT NULL constraints, but polymorphic queries require JOINs across all subtables. **TABLE_PER_CLASS** gives each concrete class its own table with all fields duplicated \u2014 no JOINs for single-type queries, but polymorphic queries require UNION ALL.\n\nMost developers default to SINGLE_TABLE without considering the consequences. If `Vehicle` has subclasses `Car` (with `numberOfDoors`) and `Truck` (with `payloadCapacity`), SINGLE_TABLE puts both columns in the same table \u2014 but neither can have a NOT NULL constraint because a Car row has no `payloadCapacity`. As the hierarchy grows, the table accumulates sparse columns.\n\n**`@MappedSuperclass` is the fourth option** and it's not an inheritance strategy at all \u2014 it shares fields across entities without creating a JPA hierarchy. No polymorphic queries possible, but also no strategy trade-offs."},
    {"type":"comparison_table","title":"Three Strategies Compared \u2014 Performance, Constraints, and Polymorphic Queries",
     "content":"The choice depends on whether you need polymorphic queries and NOT NULL constraints.\n\n| Aspect | SINGLE_TABLE | JOINED | TABLE_PER_CLASS |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Tables** | One for entire hierarchy | Parent + one per subclass | One per concrete class |\n| **Discriminator** | Required | Optional | Not used |\n| **NOT NULL on subclass columns** | No \u2014 all nullable | Yes | Yes |\n| **Polymorphic query** | Fast \u2014 single table scan | Slow \u2014 LEFT JOIN to all subtables | Slow \u2014 UNION ALL across tables |\n| **Single-type query** | Fast with discriminator filter | Fast \u2014 query subtable directly | Fast \u2014 query single table |\n| **Insert** | One INSERT | Two INSERTs (parent + sub) | One INSERT |\n| **Best for** | Small hierarchies, frequent polymorphic queries | Deep hierarchies with constraints | Rarely queried polymorphically |\n\n**JOINED generates LEFT JOINs for polymorphic queries.** `SELECT v FROM Vehicle v` becomes `SELECT ... FROM vehicle v LEFT JOIN car c ON v.id = c.id LEFT JOIN truck t ON v.id = t.id`. With 10 subclasses, that's 10 LEFT JOINs on every polymorphic query. Use JOINED only when NOT NULL constraints on subclass columns are essential."},
],
    "JPA has three inheritance mapping strategies, each with a core trade-off.\n\n**SINGLE_TABLE** is the default and the fastest for polymorphic queries \u2014 everything in one table. But every subclass-specific column must be nullable because a Car row has no Truck columns. As hierarchies grow, the table fills with sparse nulls.\n\n**JOINED** normalizes properly \u2014 shared fields in a parent table, subclass fields in subtables. You get NOT NULL constraints. But polymorphic queries LEFT JOIN every subtable \u2014 10 subclasses means 10 JOINs.\n\n**TABLE_PER_CLASS** gives each class its own table. No JOINs for single-type queries, but polymorphic queries require UNION ALL across all tables.\n\n`@MappedSuperclass` is the fourth option that isn't an inheritance strategy \u2014 it shares fields without creating a JPA hierarchy. No polymorphic queries, but no strategy trade-offs either.\n\nIn practice, I use SINGLE_TABLE for small hierarchies and JOINED when subclass columns need database-level NOT NULL enforcement.",
    "- **SINGLE_TABLE forces all subclass columns to be nullable** \u2014 a Car row has no `payloadCapacity` column value; NOT NULL constraints on subclass-specific fields are impossible with this strategy.\n- **JOINED generates LEFT JOINs for polymorphic queries** \u2014 `SELECT v FROM Vehicle v` joins every subtable; 10 subclasses means 10 LEFT JOINs per query; use only when NOT NULL constraints justify the cost.\n- **TABLE_PER_CLASS requires UNION ALL for polymorphic queries** \u2014 each concrete class has its own table with all columns duplicated; fast for single-type queries, slow for `findAll()` across the hierarchy.\n- **@MappedSuperclass isn't an inheritance strategy** \u2014 it shares fields across entities at the Java level but doesn't create a JPA hierarchy; no polymorphic queries possible, no discriminator column.\n- **Discriminator column is only required for SINGLE_TABLE** \u2014 JOINED can use it optionally for type identification; TABLE_PER_CLASS doesn't use it because each table maps to exactly one concrete class."
)

